.TH NEXCAGE 1 "October 2026" "nexcage 0.13.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nexcage \- manage LXC containers on a Proxmox VE host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nexcage
[\fB\-\-debug\fR]
[\fB\-\-log\-level\fR \fIlevel\fR]
[\fB\-\-log\-file\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-\-log\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-\-log\-format\fR \fBtext\fR|\fBjson\fR]
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-\-root\fR \fIdir\fR]
[\fB\-\-runtime\fR \fBlxc\fR|\fBcrun\fR]
\fIcommand\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nexcage
creates, starts, stops, deletes and inspects LXC containers on a Proxmox VE
host through
.BR pct (1)
and
.BR pvesh (1).
Containers are created from Proxmox templates, OCI bundle directories or, on
Proxmox VE 9.1 and later, OCI registry images.
.PP
Containers are addressed by name, which becomes the container hostname and
must be unique in the cluster. VMIDs are allocated with
.BR "pvesh get /cluster/nextid" .
.B nexcage
must run as root.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBcreate \-\-name\fR \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-node\fR \fInode\fR] [\fB\-\-storage\fR \fIstorage\fR] \fIimage\fR, \fBcreate\fR \fIid\fR \fB\-\-bundle\fR \fIdir\fR [\fB\-\-console\-socket\fR \fIpath\fR] [\fB\-\-pid\-file\fR \fIpath\fR]
Create a container.
.I image
is a template volume such as
.BR local:vztmpl/debian-12-standard_12.7-1_amd64.tar.zst ,
a template file name, an absolute path to an OCI bundle directory, or an OCI
registry reference such as
.B docker.io/library/redis:7
(Proxmox VE 9.1+).
\fB\-\-node\fR creates the container on another node of the cluster, from a
\fIstorage\fR:vztmpl/\fIfile\fR template that node can read (Proxmox LXC
backend only).
\fB\-\-storage\fR is the storage a registry image is found on or pulled to,
default local; the root filesystem goes on \fBproxmox.storage\fR.
\fB\-\-console\-socket\fR and \fB\-\-pid\-file\fR need the crun backend (\fB\-\-runtime crun\fR):
the Proxmox LXC backend refuses them, because \fBpct create\fR starts no
process. The crun backend is not built into this package's binary; it is in
the separate \-crun release binary.
.TP
\fBrun \-\-name\fR \fIname\fR \fIimage\fR
Create a container and start it.
.TP
\fBstart\fR \fIname\fR
Start a container.
.TP
\fBstop\fR \fIname\fR
Shut a container down cleanly; it is stopped forcibly after 60 seconds.
.TP
\fBdelete\fR \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]
Destroy a stopped container and remove its state. With \fB\-\-force\fR, shut a
running container down first, as \fBrunc delete \-\-force\fR does.
.TP
\fBkill\fR \fIname\fR [\fIsignal\fR], \fBkill \-s\fR \fIsignal\fR [\fB\-\-all\fR] \fIname\fR
Send a signal (default SIGTERM) to the container's init process from the host.
The signal is a name in any case, with or without SIG, or a number from 1 to 64.
Init receives it only if it handles that signal, except SIGKILL and SIGSTOP;
SIGKILL always stops the container. \fB\-\-all\fR is accepted for runc
compatibility and does not walk the cgroup: only SIGKILL reaches every process.
.TP
\fBexec\fR \fIname\fR \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR...], \fBexec\fR \fIname\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR...]
Run a command inside a running container and exit with its status, as an OCI
runtime does. Use \-\- where the command starts with a dash. The container must
be running and the command must exist in its image.
.TP
\fBlist\fR
List containers, tab-separated: ID, IMAGE, COMMAND, CREATED, STATUS, BACKEND, NODE, NAMES.
.TP
\fBstate\fR \fIname\fR|\fIvmid\fR
Print OCI runtime state as JSON: status created until the container is first
started through nexcage, and the host PID of its init while it is running or
paused on this host; 0 otherwise, also for a container on another node of the
cluster, which the io.cageforge.nexcage.node annotation names. Fails if the
container does not exist.
.TP
\fBpause\fR \fIname\fR, \fBresume\fR \fIname\fR
Freeze or thaw every process in a container through the cgroup freezer.
Nothing is written to disk; this is not \fBpct suspend\fR. On the Proxmox LXC
backend the container must be on this host. \fBstate\fR reports a frozen
container as paused; \fBpct status\fR still says running.
.TP
\fBupdate\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fIname\fR, \fBupdate \-\-resources\fR \fIfile\fR \fIname\fR
Change a container's resource limits, with the options of \fBcrun update\fR,
such as \fB\-\-memory\fR, \fB\-\-memory\-swap\fR, \fB\-\-cpu\-quota\fR,
\fB\-\-cpu\-period\fR and \fB\-\-cpu\-share\fR, or with a runtime-spec
linux.resources document in \fIfile\fR (\fB\-\fR for standard input). The
Proxmox LXC backend applies only memory, swap, a CPU limit and a CPU weight,
the settings \fBpct set\fR has, and refuses any other setting by name.
.TP
\fBsnapshot\fR \fIname\fR \fIsnapshot\fR [\fB\-\-description\fR \fItext\fR]
Take a snapshot of a container's volumes through Proxmox VE. The storage must
be one that can snapshot, such as zfs, lvm-thin or qcow2; a raw volume on a
directory storage cannot. The crun backend refuses snapshot, snapshots,
rollback and delsnapshot.
.TP
\fBsnapshots\fR [\fB\-\-format json\fR|\fBtable\fR] \fIname\fR
List a container's snapshots, oldest first, tab-separated: NAME, CREATED (UTC),
DESCRIPTION. \fB\-\-format json\fR prints an array of objects with name,
created, description and parent.
.TP
\fBrollback\fR \fIname\fR \fIsnapshot\fR [\fB\-\-start\fR]
Roll a container's volumes back to a snapshot. Proxmox stops a running
container first; it stays stopped unless \fB\-\-start\fR is given.
.TP
\fBdelsnapshot\fR \fIname\fR \fIsnapshot\fR
Delete a snapshot. A snapshot that does not exist is an error.
.TP
\fBimages\fR [\fB\-\-node\fR \fInode\fR]
List the container templates the cluster can create from, tab-separated: NODE,
STORAGE, SHARED, SIZE, TEMPLATE. A shared storage is listed once. With
\fB\-\-node\fR, only the templates that node can see.
.TP
\fBpull\fR \fIreference\fR [\fB\-\-node\fR \fInode\fR] [\fB\-\-storage\fR \fIstorage\fR] [\fB\-\-filename\fR \fIfile\fR]
Fetch an OCI image from a registry into a Proxmox storage as a container
template and print its volid, which \fBcreate\fR takes. The default node is
this host and the default storage is local. Needs Proxmox VE 9.1 or later.
.TP
\fBrmi\fR \fItemplate\fR [\fB\-\-node\fR \fInode\fR]
Remove a container template, given as \fIstorage\fR:vztmpl/\fIfile\fR, from
its storage on this host or on \fInode\fR. From a shared storage it is removed
for every node. A template that does not exist is an error.
.TP
\fBfeatures\fR
Print the OCI runtime features document.
.TP
\fBps\fR [\fB\-\-format json\fR|\fBtable\fR] \fIname\fR
Print the host PIDs in a container's cgroup.
.PP
Only the crun backend answers \fBfeatures\fR and \fBps\fR; the Proxmox LXC
backend refuses them.
.TP
\fBhealth\fR
Check pct, storage, the network, the configuration file
(\fI/etc/nexcage/config.json\fR, else \fI./config.json\fR) and the nexcage
process, print a report on standard error, and exit 1 if any check failed;
warnings do not fail. The Proxmox API is not checked: its line in the report
is always a warning.
.TP
\fBversion\fR, \fBhelp\fR
Print the version or the list of commands.
.PP
Every command accepts
.BR \-\-help .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Log at debug level and print startup and system information.
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-level\fR \fIlevel\fR
One of trace, debug, info (default), warn, error, fatal.
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-file\fR \fIpath\fR
Also write to
.I path
(and to standard error) a startup line naming the version, a line when the
command starts and two when it succeeds; with \fB\-\-debug\fR, system
information and the command's environment (debug mode, log file, timestamp)
too. The command's own log lines, including those that explain a failure, are
not written there: they go to standard error, or to the file given with
\fB\-\-log\fR. The NEXCAGE_LOG_FILE environment variable and the configuration
keys \fBlog_file\fR and \fBruntime.log_path\fR do the same.
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR \fIpath\fR
Write the runtime's own log lines to
.I path
instead of standard error, as an OCI runtime does. The one-line failure
summary still goes to standard error.
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-format\fR \fBtext\fR|\fBjson\fR
The format of the runtime's own log lines, the ones \fB\-\-log\fR redirects:
\fBjson\fR writes one object per line. Any value other than \fBjson\fR means
\fBtext\fR. The \fB\-\-log\-file\fR lines are always text.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fIpath\fR
Read configuration from
.I path
only. A missing or invalid file is an error.
.TP
\fB\-\-root\fR \fIdir\fR
Keep per-container state under
.I dir
instead of /run/nexcage. An OCI runtime takes this from its caller, so that two
callers on one host do not see each other's containers. Must be absolute.
.TP
\fB\-\-runtime\fR \fBlxc\fR|\fBcrun\fR
The backend for this command, overriding the configuration's routing.
\fBproxmox\-lxc\fR is the same as \fBlxc\fR. The crun backend is not built into
this package's binary, so a command sent to it fails.
.TP
.B \-\-systemd\-cgroup
Accepted so that a container engine is not refused. Given after \fBcreate\fR,
it is passed to libcrun on the crun backend; anywhere else it has no effect.
.TP
.B \-\-version
In place of a command, the same output as the \fBversion\fR command.
.PP
Log lines go to standard error, or to the \fB\-\-log\fR file; standard output
carries only command output.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Without \fB\-\-config\fR, the first of these files that exists and is not an
OCI runtime spec (one with a top-level ociVersion) is read; a file that does
not parse is an error:
.IR ./config.json ,
.IR /etc/nexcage/config.json ,
.IR /etc/nexcage/nexcage.json .
.TP
.B network.bridge
Bridge for eth0, which uses DHCP. Default vmbr0.
.TP
.B proxmox.storage
Storage for the root filesystem, for example local-lvm. When unset, pct uses
its default storage, local.
.TP
.B proxmox.rootfs_size_gb
Root filesystem size in GiB when
.B proxmox.storage
is set. Default 8.
.TP
.B proxmox.unprivileged
Create unprivileged containers. Default: true. Images from a registry always
run unprivileged.
.TP
.B proxmox.ostype
OS type passed to pct. Default: detected from the template.
.TP
.BR log_level ", " runtime.log_level
debug, info, warn or error; any other value is read as info. debug has the
effect of \fB\-\-debug\fR. Where both keys are set, log_level wins.
.TP
.BR log_file ", " runtime.log_path
The same as \fB\-\-log\-file\fR: not the runtime's log, which \fB\-\-log\fR
sets. Where both keys are set, log_file wins.
.TP
.B runtime.routing
Rules that pick a container's backend by its name, such as
{"pattern": "web\-*", "runtime": "crun"}. The first rule that matches wins, and
\fB\-\-runtime\fR overrides them. Default: the Proxmox LXC backend. A pattern
is a glob unless it starts with ^ or ends with $, so \&.* matches nothing; use
*. Those two make it a limited regular expression, and one that starts with ^
must match the whole name: ^web\- matches web\- alone, ^web\-.* matches web\-1.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage, such as an unknown command or a missing argument.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/nexcage/config.json
Configuration.
.TP
.I <root>/<name>/state.json
State, under \fB\-\-root\fR (default /run/nexcage), written by create, start,
stop, pause, resume and rollback, and removed by delete.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/nexcage/examples/config.json
Example configuration.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
nexcage create \-\-name web\-1 local:vztmpl/debian\-12\-standard_12.7\-1_amd64.tar.zst
nexcage start web\-1
nexcage state web\-1
nexcage stop web\-1
nexcage delete web\-1
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pct (1),
.BR pvesh (1),
.BR pveam (1)
.PP
https://github.com/CageForge/nexcage
